# netsurf: an explicit min-width turns off a flex item's automatic minimum (patch 345, with 345-libcss)
# Generated 2026-10-30
# Base: seleron/vitabrowser (patch stack through 344, with 345-libcss)
#
# CSS Flexbox 4.5: the automatic minimum size (min-content) exists only
# for `min-width: auto`. layout_flex.c applied the min-content floor in
# three places (base size, violations, used width) whatever the item's
# min-width was. `min-width: 0`, the usual way to let a flex item shrink
# below its content, did nothing. GitHub's repo column is `flex: 1 1 0;
# width: 100%; min-width: 1px` with long nowrap file rows: it kept its
# whole min-content, filled the wrapping row, and the About sidebar
# wrapped below the file list.
#
#  - layout_flex.c: in a row, when css_computed_min_width_is_auto()
#    (345-libcss) is false, the content minimum is not used as a floor.
#    min_main carries the explicit value as before.
#  - The fixes176 fallback replaced any base size <= 0 with the item's
#    intrinsic size, including a definite `flex-basis: 0`, so `flex: 1 1
#    0` behaved like `flex: 1 1 auto`. A definite zero basis now stays 0.
#    AUTO bases, and a percentage basis against an unknown container,
#    still fall back.
#
# Gate: CSS-flex-min-width-explicit (FLEXMINW345 3/3, Chromium 3/3):
# min-width 1px and 0 share the line (696 / pane at 696), and min-width
# auto keeps the content minimum and wraps. Before the patch legs 1-2
# wrapped.
